{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793401649,"nonce":"4xSz2AGAVmCMNcTTHDSBiVJOfYo=","title":"A lazy compile that panics leaves its first-compile mark, and every later call of the function hangs","message":"TieredBackend's compile_lazy_function marks a bead in first_compiles with None while its compile is under way, and replaces the mark through `publish` on every return path. A panic inside the compile (Cranelift's `.expect(\"Failed to declare ...\")` sites, for one) unwinds past `publish`: the thread dies, the mark stays None, and the next call of that function from native code waits on the condvar forever. The process then sleeps with no CPU use instead of failing.\n\nSeen with placed snapshot code declaring `zyntax_alloc` with an empty signature: the warm-up thread's compile of Pair$new panicked with IncompatibleSignature, and class/loop_carried_object.py hung for hours in zyntax-first-call-compile (main thread joined, first-call thread in Condvar::wait under install_lazy_compilers). The signature cause is fixed with the portable-code work; the hang on panic is not.\n\nFix shape: a guard in compile_lazy_function that, on unwind, removes the mark (or records a failure) and notifies, so waiters either compile themselves or surface the error; or abort the process on a panicking compile thread rather than leave waiters.","files":null}]}